+## 8. Fractional counterexample and exact author response to SQ15R2-01
+
+The literal unqualified universal J statement in v1 was false. R8's
+standing convention allowed nonzero FRACTIONAL ideals unless called
+integral, and TD1 explicitly constructs rho for fractional balanced
+pairs. The proof's use of nonnegative exponents and integral generators
+did not repair that missing statement hypothesis. Reviewer A supplied
+the following diagnostic after its complete blind whole audit. We
+preserve its attribution, rather than recasting it as new author work.
+
+Use the known Singer prime-power case q8,p73, as attributed existing
+context, without reproducing any preliminary construction. Center the
+known set in the accepted frame. The prime-divisor multiplier2 actually
+fixes it, so T=<2> is an allowed actual subgroup. The hand identities
+2^9=512=7*73+1 and2^3=8!=1 mod73 show |T|=9. Put F=Q(zeta_73)^T.
+The accepted TD1 supported FIELD-norm theorem, applied to2, gives
+
+    z in F^*, z*c(z)=2.
+
+It does NOT give z in O_F. The FRACTIONAL ideal J_fr=(z) satisfies
+J_fr*c(J_fr)=(2), has ordinary class order1, and has rho(J_fr,2)=0:
+in TD1's definition use this very z and the unit auxiliary ideal.
+The unqualified v1 equation(2) would then demand9<=2*2-3=1, and
+equation(3) at m1 would demand the same false inequality.
+
+This J_fr cannot be integral. If it were, z would be integral and
+z^2 would have norm4. Independently valid SQ15A at amplitude2 applies
+since2*2<=9+1 and q8>=3, giving z^2=+2 or-2. Thus J_fr^2=(2).
+The rational prime2 is unramified in F, so at every prime above2
+this would require twice an integer ideal exponent to equal1,
+impossible. This check does not assume the repaired class-order
+conclusion in order to justify the diagnostic.
